---
import TimerPage from '@/layouts/TimerPage.astro';
import Section from '@/components/Section.astro';
import { faqs } from '@/data/faqs';
import { relatedTimers, relatedPosts } from '@/data/related';
import { defaultConfigs } from '@/engine/presets';
import { tabatas } from '@/data/tabatas';
---

<TimerPage
  title="Tabata Timer – Free 20/10 x 8 Timer with Beeps"
  description="Free Tabata timer set to the classic 20 seconds work, 10 seconds rest, 8 rounds, 4 minutes. Clear work and rest beeps, your screen stays on, works offline."
  path="/tabata"
  h1="Tabata timer"
  lede="This Tabata timer runs the classic pattern: 20 seconds on, 10 seconds off, 8 rounds, 4 minutes. There's nothing to set. Just press Start."
  config={defaultConfigs.tabata}
  faq={faqs['/tabata']}
  related={relatedTimers['/tabata']!}
  posts={relatedPosts['/tabata']}
  crumbName="Tabata timer"
>
  <Section title="The Tabata protocol" flush>
    <p>
      The pattern came from Irisawa Koichi, coach of Japan's national speed-skating team. In 1996,
      Izumi Tabata and his team tested it on young, fit university PE students riding exercise
      bikes. Each 20 seconds was an all-out sprint, far harder than any pace they could hold for
      long. Over six weeks, both their stamina and their power for short, hard bursts improved.
      That's why the name stuck.
    </p>
    <p>
      The key is effort. If you can chat during the 20 seconds, you're doing gentler intervals with
      a Tabata timer. That's fine too.
    </p>
    <p>
      This page has the fewest settings on the site. Press Start and it goes straight into round
      one. For other work and rest times, pick a variant below, such as
      <a href="/tabata/30-15-8">30/15</a>. Variant pages let you change Work and Rest in seconds.
    </p>
  </Section>
  <Section title="Common variants">
    <ul class="links">
      {tabatas.map((t) => (
        <li>
          <a href={t.path}>{t.h1}</a>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </Section>
  <Section title="Sounds">
    <p>
      You'll hear a rising pair for work and a falling pair for rest. Ticks count down the last
      three seconds of each. Three notes play after the final rest to tell you you're done. The
      phase word and icon change too, in case your music is loud.
    </p>
  </Section>
</TimerPage>
